00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
the situation along ukraine's border  appears to be deteriorating in rapid  fashion with u.s intelligence agencies  now fearful that russia has amassed  large enough posture that it could act  militarily in a reasonably swift fashion  the speculation had been that any  movement by moscow could happen after  the beijing games but in warning  americans to vacate ukraine there's a  more stringent sense of urgency the  pentagon has now confirmed a secondary  deployment of military personnel to  nato's eastern flank bringing the total  numbers to around five thousand these  troops will not fight with ukrainian  soldiers or against russia but instead  defend nato territory now after a week  of shuttle diplomacy that failed to make  any breakthroughs president joe biden  convened a meeting with nato and eu  allies on friday that included the prime  minister pledging to protect ukrainian  sovereignty and consequences should  russia strike russia must de-escalate  the west is standing firm together  coordinated and ready to impose severe  costs including sanctions  if russia  invades ukraine once again we are not  saying that a decision has been taken a  final decision has been taken by  president putin what we are saying is  that we have a sufficient level of  concern based on what we are seeing on  the ground that assessment runs counter  to reporting that the kremlin has  already made a decision to invade u.s  military and intelligence officials  believe any incursion would likely  include ground and air attacks and could  target the capital of kiev but also fear  the use of cyber warfare to further  destabilize ukraine and potentially its  allies reggie shakini global news  washington
 

